/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-813-
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-814-- It binds authority to the execution request, not merely to a user object in context. It can make confused-deputy boundaries explicit. An agent cannot silently substitute another tenant ID if the handle is tenant-bound. A tool cannot widen authority by choosing a different raw resource ID.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-815-- The verifier can reject a mismatched handle before every operation. Grant attenuation can give subagents less authority than parents. A subagent cannot invent a broader branded capability. Grant-specific resource identity prevents cache/state aliasing across authority sets.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-816-- If `resource(grants=A)` and `resource(grants=B)` are distinct objects, state created under A cannot accidentally be reused under B. Capability-bound storage handles can enforce row/prefix/database selection below agent code. Tool implementations do not need to remember to add tenant filters. A missing check becomes a missing handle, not a permissive code path.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md:817:- Durable resume can require a freshly minted capability rather than restoring stale authority. HITL can mint a one-shot approval capability for exact action, parameters, tenant, and expiry. Approval then becomes authorization evidence rather than a Boolean resume payload.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-818-
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-819-## Where Mastra is closest
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-820-
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-821-- Mastra checks agent, workflow, tool, MCP tool, and memory invocation boundaries. Its `resourceMapping` translates framework identity into policy identity. Its `permissionMapping` makes verbs explicit. Its provider is called again at protected lifecycle points.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-822-- Its custom `requireActor()` can constrain autonomous actors. Route metadata co-locates object and verb with the route. These are good policy-enforcement-point properties. Mastra is materially closer than route-only API-key auth.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-823-- But authority remains a decision result, not a resource handle. After the Boolean allow, tool code may use ambient resources. `storedResources.scope` is a filter convention, not an unforgeable capability identity. Workflow `start/resume/restart` gaps show that entry-point enumeration can miss paths.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-824-- `requireForProtectedRoutes` being optional means coverage is configuration-sensitive. Trusted system actors default to bypassing user-centric `require()` after only tenant-scope presence. `requireActor()` is optional provider work. Mastra gets enforcement breadth right.
/tmp/claude-1000/-home-ubuntu-projects-boring-ui-v2/2f6c6143-73be-4a87-886b-e8dd7fb126e0/scratchpad/research/r7-fga.md-825-- It gets structural confinement wrong or leaves it outside scope.
